  "caveats": [
    "Statistical closeness to a folk corpus is not musical quality, and a difference from it is not poor quality. Nothing here says B is better; the listening judgements do.",
    "Generator A returns contrasting alternatives; Generator B returns a sequence in which phrases can develop one another. The development metrics of A therefore describe unrelated phrases placed side by side.",
    "B is steered by the development half of the corpus; the held-out columns show whether it generalises. Both sets are small.",
    "Nottingham Folk Music Database — cleaned ABC files: 23 pieces (3 in reel, 3 in jig, 3 in slip jig, 3 in hornpipe, 3 in waltz, 3 in morris, 3 in playford, 2 in ashover), by meter 7 in 4/4, 7 in 6/8, 5 in 9/8, 3 in 3/4, 1 in 2/4. The generator writes 4/4 only. A difference between repertoires is not a defect.",
    "Phrase length differs: the generator's phrases have a median of 1.75 bars (7 beats), the corpus's 2 bars (7.333 beats). The 4/4 subset (7 tunes, 63 phrases) controls for meter, not for length. The matched subsets keep only corpus phrases of 5–10 beats, the generator's own range: 117 phrases from 22 tunes, of which 49 from 7 in 4/4.",
    "Each metric carries its verdict in every subset and a stability label. \"stable\" means the generator is outside the corpus's range in the same direction in every control subset that was large enough (3+ tunes); \"repertoire\" means the subsets disagree, so the difference may only reflect which music is compared; \"unclear\" means fewer than two subsets could be judged. Stable is not proof: the subsets overlap and are small.",
    "A verdict compares the generator's pooled value with the 10th–90th percentile of individual corpus pieces: it says the value lies outside how much real tunes differ from each other, nothing about quality.",
    "The sequence metrics (continuity and development) look at neighbouring phrases inside one tune or run. They depend on where the phrase boundaries are, which for the corpus are heuristic hypotheses; the numbers of neighbouring pairs per subset are in the subset table (166 for the whole corpus, 78 matched, 35 matched in 4/4).",
    "The development categories are exclusive and ordered (exact repetition, modified ending, rhythm kept, melody kept). \"Melody recurs\" is a loose test: any shared three-step interval pattern counts, so stepwise music shares one easily. The exact and modified-ending tests are strict and can be zero in a small corpus; a zero there says the corpus is too small to show it, not that it never happens.",
    "The corpus holds only 2 written rests (48 more are suspected converter padding and are ignored), so it cannot say how much silence is right.",
    "Phrase boundaries in the corpus are hypotheses (see the phrase analysis); the generator's phrases are exactly what it was asked to build.",
    "Nobody has listened to either side for this comparison; no listening evaluation has been done.",
    "Only 7 corpus tunes are in 4/4, so the 4/4 spreads rest on very few pieces."
  ]
}
